target:    tests/bench/lib/export-snapshot.php
gate:      tests/docker/run-rollup-floor.sh (lane: fp_live_mutations, cell 80)
kind:      restore-defect
expect:    CHAINED HASH differs
precondition: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM wp_slim_stats WHERE CAST(resource AS BINARY) <> CAST(UPPER(resource) AS BINARY)
rationale: NOT A REGISTRY ENTRY, and deliberately so — its gate is a live MySQL-to-SQLite round
           trip inside a docker cell, which `composer test:mutations` cannot run and must not be
           made to depend on. It lives here, in the format the registry uses, so that the live
           lane's mutations are pre-declared files a reviewer can read rather than sed
           expressions buried in a shell array.

           WHAT IT PROVES. The registered sibling (S3-export-collapses-null-to-empty-01) is a
           NULL defect: it is caught by whichever side treats NULL specially, and the corpus
           must contain a NULL for it to fire at all. This one is pure byte corruption on a
           value that IS present — every lowercase letter in every exported string becomes
           uppercase on the way into SQLite — so it fires on ordinary data and it fires in the
           one direction the export's whole design is about: SQLite must not reinterpret the
           bytes MySQL held. The PHP-side chained hash is computed from the ROW, not from what
           was stored, so the failure localises exactly as the gate's diagnostic claims:
           mysql == php != python, "the drift is in the export write or the Python re-encode".

           WHY strtoupper AND NOT A ONE-BYTE FLIP. A single flipped byte in a single row is the
           weaker mutation: it can only be observed if that row is exported, so a gate that
           exported ten rows and stopped would still catch it while proving nothing about the
           other 29,990. Corrupting every string makes the mutation's reach the corpus's reach,
           and CONTROL 2 already performs the single-byte case in process, on the real export.

           PRECONDITION. Measured before the diff is applied, and measured in BYTES. The first
           version read `resource REGEXP '[a-z]'`, which MySQL evaluates under the column's
           case-insensitive collation and therefore matches an all-uppercase corpus — reporting
           "precondition satisfied" for data on which strtoupper() is a no-op. The predicate now
           is literally "strtoupper would change this value". A mutation that cannot fire is
           indistinguishable from a gate that works, which is the shape PITFALLS 44 records, and
           a precondition that cannot fail is that same shape one layer out.
---
diff --git a/tests/bench/lib/export-snapshot.php b/tests/bench/lib/export-snapshot.php
--- a/tests/bench/lib/export-snapshot.php
+++ b/tests/bench/lib/export-snapshot.php
@@ -143,7 +143,7 @@ if (!function_exists('slimstat_fp2_export_snapshot')) {
                     } else {
                         // Strings, and integers too wide for SQLite's signed 64-bit INTEGER:
                         // raw bytes, which SQLite must not reinterpret.
-                        $insert->bindValue($i + 1, (string) $value, SQLITE3_BLOB);
+                        $insert->bindValue($i + 1, strtoupper((string) $value), SQLITE3_BLOB);
                     }
                 }
                 $insert->execute();
